            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Applicable Scenarios and Prerequisites</h2>
<p>This guide is designed for cross-border e-commerce DTC site teams with monthly ad budgets ranging from $10K to $500K. If you've recently launched your site and are ready to scale advertising, or if your current ROAS remains unsatisfactory, this budget allocation SOP will help you establish a clear framework quickly.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Prerequisites</strong>: You have active accounts on at least two of the following platforms—Google Ads, Meta Ads (Facebook/Instagram), or TikTok Ads—and have a need for site traffic acquisition.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Determine Total Monthly Budget</h2>
<h3>1.1 Calculate Maximum Investable Budget</h3>
<p>Before allocating, clarify two figures:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monthly marketing budget</strong>: The upper limit your company can afford for advertising</li>
<li><strong>Safety buffer</strong>: Reserve 10%-15% as contingency for emergency scaling or unexpected traffic opportunities</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, if a tool product team's monthly marketing budget is $50,000, the actual allocable budget would be 50,000 × 85% ≈ $42,500.</p>
<h3>1.2 Divide by Marketing Objectives</h3>
<p>Split your allocable budget into three funding pools:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Funding Pool</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
<th>Recommended %</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Brand Awareness</td>
<td>Brand search, YouTube display ads</td>
<td>10%-15%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Performance/Conversion</td>
<td>Search ads, shopping ads, dynamic retargeting</td>
<td>60%-70%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Testing/Exploration</td>
<td>New channel tests, new creative validation</td>
<td>15%-25%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Why this split?</strong> The performance pool is your foundation and must be protected. The testing pool determines your growth ceiling. The brand awareness pool builds a moat against competitors.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Channel Budget Allocation</h2>
<h3>2.1 Primary Channel Selection Logic</h3>
<p>Core channels for DTC site traffic typically include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Google Ads</strong>: Ideal for users with clear purchase intent; short conversion path</li>
<li><strong>Meta Ads</strong>: Great for interest-based targeting and viral potential; creative-driven</li>
<li><strong>TikTok Ads</strong>: Suitable for younger demographics; high growth potential with short video</li>
<li><strong>Email Retargeting</strong>: Low cost, high ROI; usually excluded from ad budget but requires tool allocation</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Budget Distribution Ratio Reference</h3>
<p>Based on DTC operations experience, use these ratios as an initial benchmark:</p>
<pre><code>Google Ads: 35%-45%
Meta Ads: 35%-45%
TikTok Ads: 10%-20%
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Key Principle</strong>: Avoid equal distribution. Concentrate 60%+ of your budget on the 1-2 most stable performing channels, using the remainder for exploration.</p>
<h3>2.3 Sub-Campaign Budget Breakdown</h3>
<p>Using Google Ads as an example, 40% of your budget can be further split as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Search campaigns: 25% (high-intent keywords)</li>
<li>Shopping campaigns: 50% (core product promotion)</li>
<li>Display remarketing: 15% (流失用户召回)</li>
<li>P-Max campaigns: 10% (smart bidding tests)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Testing Phase vs. Scaling Phase Adjustments</h2>
<h3>3.1 Testing Phase (First 4 Weeks) Strategy</h3>
<p>When launching new accounts or entering new markets, adopt a <strong>small budget, broad reach</strong> approach:</p>
<ol>
<li>Set minimum budget for each campaign (typically 50% of that channel's daily budget)</li>
<li>Launch 3-5 ad groups simultaneously for creative or audience testing</li>
<li>Record CPC, CPA, and ROAS data daily</li>
<li>Week 2: Gradually pause the bottom 20% of underperforming ad groups</li>
<li>By Week 4: Confirm primary creative assets and audience packages</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Key Threshold</strong>: If CPA exceeds 150% of your target with fewer than 10 conversions, pause that ad group.</p>
<h3>3.2 Scaling Phase (Week 5+) Strategy</h3>
<p>Once stabilized, shift budget toward data-validated high-performing channels:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Performance Indicator</th>
<th>Budget Adjustment</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>ROAS &gt; 120% of target</td>
<td>Increase that channel's budget by 20%-30%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROAS at target (100%-120%)</td>
<td>Maintain current budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROAS &lt; 80% of target</td>
<td>Reduce budget by 20% while optimizing creative or audience</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Two consecutive weeks of ROAS &lt; 60%</td>
<td>Pause the campaign; conduct a review</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Establish Weekly Review Mechanism</h2>
<h3>4.1 Data That Must Appear in Weekly Reports</h3>
<p>Output a budget execution report each weekend, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Actual spend vs. planned spend by channel</li>
<li>Overall ROAS and week-over-week changes</li>
<li>Top 3 best-performing ad creatives</li>
<li>Ad groups needing optimization or pause</li>
<li>Budget adjustment recommendations for next week</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Triggers for Budget Reallocation</h3>
<p>Immediately initiate a budget reallocation meeting when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Single-channel ROAS declines more than 15% consecutively</li>
<li>Policy changes cause mass creative rejections</li>
<li>Significant cart abandonment or refund surge on site</li>
<li>Competitor aggressive scaling drives up CPM/CPC</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Execution Checklist</h2>
<p>After completing this SOP, confirm the following are in place:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Monthly total budget divided into three funding pools</li>
<li>[ ] Google, Meta, and TikTok budget ratios confirmed</li>
<li>[ ] Daily budgets set for each ad campaign</li>
<li>[ ] 4-week testing phase data tracking sheet established</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly report template created with reminders set</li>
<li>[ ] Emergency budget adjustment triggers aligned with team</li>
</ul>
<p>After executing this SOP for 2-3 months, you'll have accumulated sufficient data to support more precise budget decisions.</p>
